 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Adaptive Radius Clamping**: If the Gaussian opacity $\sigma$ is high or $\alpha_{low}$ is low, the adaptive radius $r_{ad}$ may exceed the original radius $r_o$ (based on the 99% confidence interval). In this case, the final radius $r$ must be clamped to $r_o$ to ensure the rendering range does not exceed the standard confidence interval [S1].
-*   **Degenerate Gaussians**: If the projected covariance matrix is singular or near-singular, the ellipse coefficients may be invalid. The pseudocode includes a check for positive eigenvalues and a fallback to the radius $r$ [S1, G].
+*   **Adaptive Radius Clamping**: If the Gaussian opacity $\sigma$ is high or $\alpha_{low}$ is low, the adaptive radius $r_{ad}$ may exceed the original radius $r_o$ (based on the 99% confidence interval) [S1]. In this case, the final radius $r$ must be clamped to $r_o$ to ensure the rendering range does not exceed the standard confidence interval [S1].
+*   **Degenerate Gaussians**: If the projected covariance matrix is singular or near-singular, the ellipse coefficients may be invalid [G]. The implementation must check the determinant and eigenvalues before computing radii or AABB bounds [G].
 
